{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1069 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "a71171b61604d23087aeb5f9358249fd42222524210df2e2eb200b2d85c3778f" :edb/db-before-t 1068 :edb/db-after-t 1069 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534381 50 #inst "2026-10-06T18:52:37.526Z" 13194139534381 true] [17592186183885 1145 "First, we convert the inequality into slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+2y & <4 \\\\ 2y & <-x+4 \\\\ y & <-\\frac{1}{2}x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed as usual. We start by drawing the corresponding straight line \n\n$$\ny=-\\dfrac{1}{2}x+2.\n$$\n\nSince the inequality involves a 'strictly less than' sign, the line $y=-\\dfrac{1}{2}x+2$ itself is *not* part of the solution. Therefore, we draw a dashed line.\n\nThen, we must decide whether to shade above or below the line. The inequality \n\n$$\ny < -\\dfrac{1}{2}x+2\n$$\n\nrefers to the $y$-values that are *less than* those on the line $y = -\\dfrac{1}{2}x+2,$ so we shade *below* the line.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/assets/1450257c533c670d3ce0d803aa62b75c2ffe44159e26a3daf5dd3c04026a6fe4.png)" 13194139534381 true] [17592186183885 1145 "First, we convert the inequality into slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x + 2y & < 4 \\\\ 2y & < - x + 4 \\\\ y & < - \\frac{1}{2}x + 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed as usual. We start by drawing the corresponding straight line\n\n$$\ny=-\\dfrac{1}{2}x+2\n$$\n\nSince the inequality involves a 'strictly less than' sign, the line\n\n$$\ny=-\\dfrac{1}{2}x+2\n$$\n\nitself is *not* part of the solution. Therefore, we draw a dashed line.\n\nThen, we must decide whether to shade above or below the line. The inequality\n\n$$\ny < -\\dfrac{1}{2}x+2\n$$\n\nrefers to the $y$-values that are *less than* those on the line\n\n$$\ny = -\\dfrac{1}{2}x+2\n$$\n\nso we shade *below* the line.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3822/Source/Images/eea9bbe3f74a8d4df15c497ee51991b6.png)" 13194139534381 false] [17592186183886 1145 "First, we convert the inequality into slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-x-y & \\le -1 \\\\ -y & \\le x-1 \\\\ y & \\ge -x+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed as usual. We start by drawing the corresponding straight line \n\n$$\ny=-x+1.\n$$\n\n Since the inequality involves a 'greater than *or equal to*' sign, the line $y=-x+1$ itself is part of the solution. Therefore, we draw a *solid line*.\n\nThen, we must decide whether to shade above or below the line. The inequality \n\n$$\ny \\geq -x+1\n$$\n\n refers to the $y$-values that are *greater than* or equal to those on the line $y=-x+1,$ so we shade *above* the line. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/assets/a649958285f52e9d61c665bf4cd58844e4ed7cea1cf9e0f949efe5a0f25e3263.png)" 13194139534381 true] [17592186183886 1145 "First, we convert the inequality into slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-x - y & \\le - 1 \\\\ -y & \\le x - 1 \\\\ y & \\ge - x + 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed as usual. We start by drawing the corresponding straight line\n\n$$\ny=-x+1\n$$\n\nSince the inequality involves a 'greater than *or equal to*' sign, the line\n\n$$\ny=-x+1\n$$\n\nitself is part of the solution. Therefore, we draw a *solid line*.\n\nThen, we must decide whether to shade above or below the line. The inequality\n\n$$\ny \\geq -x+1\n$$\n\nrefers to the $y$-values that are *greater than* or equal to those on the line\n\n$$\ny=-x+1\n$$\n\nso we shade *above* the line.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3822/Source/Images/c0cf262705b842b59fc8f287c310b7c8.png)" 13194139534381 false] [17592186183888 1100 "Find the inequality that describes the shaded region shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/assets/677e2257e2cb3a5706b7dc2d1088fc68ff12a2c39d976819d52f25fb2dfbe77d.png)" 13194139534381 true] [17592186183888 1100 "Find the inequality that describes the shaded region shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3822/Source/Images/43fad45f1d96c5e8626ab609a68bfd59.png)" 13194139534381 false] [17592186183888 1145 "The border-line is a vertical line passing through the point $(-1,0)$. Therefore, the line has the equation $x=-1$.\n\nNow, we need to decide which inequality symbol to use.\n\n- Since the line is *dashed*, we will use a *strict* inequality.\n- Since the shaded region is to the *right* of the line, the inequality refers to the $x$-values that are *greater than* those on the line $x=-1$.\n\nTherefore, the corresponding inequality is\n$x > -1$." 13194139534381 false] [17592186183888 1145 "The border-line is a vertical line passing through the point $(-1,0).$ Therefore, the line has the equation \n\n$$\nx=-1.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to decide which inequality symbol to use. \n\n- Since the line is *dashed*, we will use a *strict* inequality.\n\n- Since the shaded region is to the *right* of the line, the inequality refers to the $x$-values that are *greater than* those on the line $x=-1.$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding inequality is\n\n$$\nx > -1.\n$$" 13194139534381 true] [17592186184081 1020 #edb/ref 17592186285070 13194139534381 true] [17592186184081 1020 #edb/ref 17592186285091 13194139534381 true] [17592186184081 1020 #edb/ref 17592186285144 13194139534381 true] [17592186184081 1020 #edb/ref 17592186285151 13194139534381 true] [17592186184082 1020 #edb/ref 17592186285063 13194139534381 true] [17592186184082 1020 #edb/ref 17592186285077 13194139534381 true] [17592186184082 1020 #edb/ref 17592186285098 13194139534381 true] [17592186184082 1020 #edb/ref 17592186285105 13194139534381 true] [17592186184082 1020 #edb/ref 17592186285112 13194139534381 true] [17592186184083 1020 #edb/ref 17592186285084 13194139534381 true] [17592186184083 1020 #edb/ref 17592186285119 13194139534381 true] [17592186184083 1020 #edb/ref 17592186285128 13194139534381 true] [17592186184083 1020 #edb/ref 17592186285137 13194139534381 true] [17592186184679 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534381 true] [17592186184679 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/assets/dcfffb2c9005da34a529cdf5ef372bdac840a1308b92507ad0ea0a5359a1387f.png)\n\nFind the inequality that describes the shaded region shown above." 13194139534381 true] [17592186184679 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3822/Source/Images/q-29423.png)\n\nFind the inequality that describes the shaded region shown above." 13194139534381 false] [17592186184679 1145 "The border-line is a vertical line passing through the point $(-4,0).$ Therefore, the line has the equation $x=-4.$\n\nNow, we need to decide which inequality symbol to use. Since the line is dashed, we will use a strict inequality. Since the shaded region is to the *right* of the line, the inequality refers to the $x$-values that are *greater than* those on the line $x=-4.$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding inequality is\n\n$$\nx>-4.\n$$" 13194139534381 true] [17592186184763 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534381 true] [17592186184763 1100 "Which of the following shaded areas describes the region bounded by the inequality $x - y < 3$?" 13194139534381 false] [17592186184763 1100 "Which of the following shaded areas describes the region bounded by the inequality $x-y<3?$" 13194139534381 true] [17592186184763 1145 "First, we convert the inequality into slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-y & <3 \\\\ -y & <-x+3 \\\\ y & >x-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed as usual. We start by drawing the corresponding straight line \n\n$$\ny=x-3.\n$$\n\n Since the inequality involves a 'strictly greater than' sign, the line $y=x-3$ itself is *not* part of the solution. Therefore, we draw a **dashed line**.\n\nThen, we need to decide whether to shade above or below the line. The inequality \n\n$$\ny>x-3\n$$\n\n refers to the $y$-values that are *greater than* those on the line $y=x-3,$ so we shade *above* the line.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/assets/77a4c9b6bc705a2fbf486bfef5a6ce57924aae86c4abf1510040c7957b873329.png)" 13194139534381 true] [17592186285063 1017 #edb/ref 17592186285064 13194139534381 true] [17592186285063 1089 "q-109940" 13194139534381 true] [17592186285063 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534381 true] [17592186285063 1100 "Which of the following shaded areas describes the region bounded by the inequality $-10x+5y\\ge -5?$" 13194139534381 true] [17592186285063 1113 #uuid "187bfe25-9849-538d-9d2d-ce122bf58749" 13194139534381 true] [17592186285063 1145 "First, we convert the inequality into slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-10x+5y & \\ge -5 \\\\ 5y & \\ge 10x-5 \\\\ y & \\ge 2x-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed as usual. We start by drawing the corresponding straight line $y=2x-1.$ Since the inequality involves a 'greater than *or equal to*' sign, the line $y=2x-1$ itself is part of the solution. Therefore, we draw a solid line.\n\nThen, we need to decide whether to shade above or below the line. The inequality $y\\ge 2x-1$ refers to the $y$-values that are *greater than* or equal to those on the line $y=2x-1,$ so we shade *above* the line.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/assets/a9eef4f75cb108bd00fa1230a4be9f5b9edfc21d0ff7f999a3ca8aeb212f08b7.png)" 13194139534381 true] [17592186285064 1000 #edb/ref 17592186285065 13194139534381 true] [17592186285064 1000 #edb/ref 17592186285066 13194139534381 true] [17592186285064 1000 #edb/ref 17592186285067 13194139534381 true] [17592186285064 1000 #edb/ref 17592186285068 13194139534381 true] [17592186285064 1000 #edb/ref 17592186285069 13194139534381 true] [17592186285064 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534381 true] [17592186285064 1041 "selection" 13194139534381 true] [17592186285064 1104 #edb/ref 17592186285067 13194139534381 true] [17592186285064 1110 #uuid "73936b03-e1a1-55ee-8794-14b8b02ef2bc" 13194139534381 true] [17592186285065 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/assets/b1fc6cc7406d0f47b142949371b66c59aac6bcdf8bdc7dbc62a113e063642fe2.png" 13194139534381 true] [17592186285065 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139534381 true] [17592186285065 1111 #uuid "17abc78a-785b-5192-8fa7-439ec6917908" 13194139534381 true] [17592186285066 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/assets/2ae1370dda61376a0c8b684a33f3722620aef05ee289f0a83591546e49221389.png" 13194139534381 true] [17592186285066 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139534381 true] [17592186285066 1111 #uuid "ce553c50-eee6-5849-a160-0b18133622f9" 13194139534381 true] [17592186285067 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/assets/419a5f107674817f111a0eed14a49cc91d007d592b61e2c0c5440967f607a356.png" 13194139534381 true] [17592186285067 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139534381 true] [17592186285067 1111 #uuid "26b3b76d-ea9a-56df-8296-b5a24c12c7a6" 13194139534381 true] [17592186285068 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/assets/2f186f14dde18a41004fd77e7280686f32bfc97c9c35f722e5c799c498a36a50.png" 13194139534381 true] [17592186285068 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139534381 true] [17592186285068 1111 #uuid "aecd5e66-a26f-5174-9950-637139443167" 13194139534381 true] [17592186285069 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/assets/bb3db9d0eaa602c87df225b21be99c0346c72ec142bb498b92671495bc77858a.png" 13194139534381 true] [17592186285069 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139534381 true] [17592186285069 1111 #uuid "d3e86715-8636-5654-ba90-c6253250ec74" 13194139534381 true] [17592186285070 1017 #edb/ref 17592186285071 13194139534381 true] [17592186285070 1089 "q-109942" 13194139534381 true] [17592186285070 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534381 true] [17592186285070 1100 "Which of the following shaded areas describes the region bounded by the inequality $5x-10y<-20?$" 13194139534381 true] [17592186285070 1113 #uuid "3626480d-f8b9-54d9-af65-fe6ef10bb1f3" 13194139534381 true] [17592186285070 1145 "First, we convert the inequality into slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x-10y & <-20 \\\\ -10y & <-5x-20 \\\\ 10y & >5x+20 \\\\ y & >\\frac{1}{2}x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed as usual. We start by drawing the corresponding straight line $y=\\frac{1}{2}x+2.$ Since the inequality involves a 'strictly greater than' sign, the line $y=\\frac{1}{2}x+2$ itself is *not* part of the solution. Therefore, we draw a dashed line.\n\nThen, we need to decide whether to shade above or below the line. The inequality $y>\\frac{1}{2}x+2$ refers to the $y$-values that are *greater than* those on the line $y=\\frac{1}{2}x+2,$ so we shade *above* the line.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/assets/351bb4b4324432e2cc747e67dac22100da3c9e5318339832efa046f504394c35.png)" 13194139534381 true] [17592186285071 1000 #edb/ref 17592186285072 13194139534381 true] [17592186285071 1000 #edb/ref 17592186285073 13194139534381 true] [17592186285071 1000 #edb/ref 17592186285074 13194139534381 true] [17592186285071 1000 #edb/ref 17592186285075 13194139534381 true] [17592186285071 1000 #edb/ref 17592186285076 13194139534381 true] [17592186285071 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534381 true] [17592186285071 1041 "selection" 13194139534381 true] [17592186285071 1104 #edb/ref 17592186285073 13194139534381 true] [17592186285071 1110 #uuid "88498f1b-dff4-5ff5-9e9f-1dfa6a2f609b" 13194139534381 true] [17592186285072 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/assets/0668624761edfaf6f67a69d6059e7b0c54acd87042ec9f7400f1d4c2dfc5ff0b.png" 13194139534381 true] [17592186285072 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139534381 true] [17592186285072 1111 #uuid "ac719246-6a3c-5b11-be2a-1ac4e31ae591" 13194139534381 true] [17592186285073 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/assets/a16d99b349797e305083d8295ef098fbfbe3ea82970b5a48606ea5c678e08262.png" 13194139534381 true] [17592186285073 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139534381 true] [17592186285073 1111 #uuid "239033c2-0a8b-51a0-b255-a4f2efa1928f" 13194139534381 true] [17592186285074 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/assets/ebf7fd57f5d07afebc22899418ce34d14e59dcdb2a01087950de675b7086f405.png" 13194139534381 true] [17592186285074 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139534381 true] [17592186285074 1111 #uuid "1780ed63-0950-59cc-97d0-725d4da92b8a" 13194139534381 true] [17592186285075 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/assets/1e408923db2c7223715b9f82a2777a411e2c79b3614320975c237f2f503f2368.png" 13194139534381 true] [17592186285075 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139534381 true] [17592186285075 1111 #uuid "6e6eb7d3-0717-5007-b786-e41874f150b7" 13194139534381 true] [17592186285076 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/assets/458a5dd519867a662237d7b52e44627d1867f25dcceeea446203a42571f91a00.png" 13194139534381 true] [17592186285076 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139534381 true] [17592186285076 1111 #uuid "f0582487-dca4-5d7d-a854-862539ca2500" 13194139534381 true] [17592186285077 1017 #edb/ref 17592186285078 13194139534381 true] [17592186285077 1089 "q-109946" 13194139534381 true] [17592186285077 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534381 true] [17592186285077 1100 "Which of the following shaded areas describes the region bounded by the inequality $2x-y\\ge 3?$" 13194139534381 true] [17592186285077 1113 #uuid "8fc6e780-0a9e-5665-a2f1-8e50997d5b65" 13194139534381 true] [17592186285077 1145 "First, we convert the inequality into slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-y & \\ge 3 \\\\ -y & \\ge -2x+3 \\\\ y & \\le 2x-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed as usual. We start by drawing the corresponding straight line $y=2x-3.$ Since the inequality involves a 'less than *or equal to*' sign, the line $y=2x-3$ itself is part of the solution. Therefore, we draw a solid line.\n\nThen, we need to decide whether to shade above or below the line. The inequality $y\\le 2x-3$ refers to the $y$-values that are *less than* or equal to those on the line $y=2x-3,$ so we shade *below* the line.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/assets/020b2d81ed5913dfe41fb296811da30ee2c6ea4fc7ea8184335db6d866598c5a.png)" 13194139534381 true] [17592186285078 1000 #edb/ref 17592186285079 13194139534381 true] [17592186285078 1000 #edb/ref 17592186285080 13194139534381 true] [17592186285078 1000 #edb/ref 17592186285081 13194139534381 true] [17592186285078 1000 #edb/ref 17592186285082 13194139534381 true] [17592186285078 1000 #edb/ref 17592186285083 13194139534381 true] [17592186285078 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534381 true] [17592186285078 1041 "selection" 13194139534381 true] [17592186285078 1104 #edb/ref 17592186285079 13194139534381 true] [17592186285078 1110 #uuid "e4845259-70e8-54d5-8fec-ae787e49366b" 13194139534381 true] [17592186285079 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/assets/a918cfee32950317ee7621317d5af40c49ca04209b981c6e94d529c8d7896384.png" 13194139534381 true] [17592186285079 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139534381 true] [17592186285079 1111 #uuid "b3a95ac4-5aa6-5ea5-a4d1-51498103bbf4" 13194139534381 true] [17592186285080 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/assets/8104d0507b16ac8c43900e8278e8d5079f99539b56c9eb150b938751d6c57810.png" 13194139534381 true] [17592186285080 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139534381 true] [17592186285080 1111 #uuid "57